      <p>
        The pricing distribution splits into two large groups: {tierDist.entry} platforms come in under $100 per user a month once flat plans are split across a {REFERENCE_TEAM_SIZE}-person team (Jobber, Housecall Pro, Tradify, Service Autopilot, Joist), while {customQuoted} publish no price at all, a group that includes ServiceTitan, BuildOps and Procore alongside vendors such as Aspire and FieldRoutes that quote by company size or customer count. {topPricedSentence} Those two groups are counted separately here on purpose: an unpublished price is not evidence of an expensive one. The middle is sparse, with only {tierDist.mid} platform{tierDist.mid === 1 ? '' : 's'} in the $100-$249 per-user band.
      </p>

      <h2>The "custom-quoted" opacity tax</h2>

      <p>
        <strong>{customQuoted} of {tools.length} platforms ({customQuotedPct}%)</strong> publish no public pricing whatsoever. To get a number, you contact sales, usually through a demo, and receive a quote for your team size and modules. The group includes ServiceTitan, BuildOps, Procore, FieldEdge, FieldRoutes, Aspire, Simpro, ServiceTrade and BigChange.
      </p>

      <p>
        The opacity has a tax. Contractors evaluating these platforms can't comparison-shop on price without committing time to multiple sales calls. The information asymmetry favors vendors: the buyer doesn't know if their quote is fair until they've already invested significant evaluation effort. This report does not estimate their prices: they are counted as quote-only and left out of every price median on this page.
      </p>

